::JOUR_U05_L07_QUIZ_Q01::[MLA.JOUR.PUB.01] Source: Digital Publication Package. Project: The journalism team is preparing a web package called Cafeteria Changes Explained. Required parts: a short explanatory article, one accurate feature photo, a three-image sequence showing the lunch line process, a 30-second audio caption, and a publication checklist. Audience: students and families who need clear, accurate information before Monday. Visual Accuracy Concern: one photo shows an old menu board that is no longer used. Workflow Concern: the package must be reviewed, captioned, exported, and submitted by Friday at 3:00 p.m. Publication Goal: the final package must use accurate visuals, clear sequence, coherent digital presentation, and an organized online layout. Question: Which choice best protects publication readiness? {
~A. Publish because the package has several parts#Not correct. Step 1: reread the embedded source instead of relying on a general preference. Step 2: locate the audience need, accuracy issue, digital tool, workflow, or publication requirement. Step 3: reject choices that weaken verification, visual accuracy, collaboration, sequence, deadline control, or publication readiness.
=B. Choose the option that verifies facts, uses accurate visuals, orders media clearly, and meets the deadline#Correct. Step 1: connect the decision to the MLA journalism standard shown in the item. Step 2: use the embedded source to verify the audience need, accuracy issue, digital tool, workflow, or publication requirement. Step 3: choose the option that protects credible, clear, publication-ready journalism.
~C. Let the strongest visual replace all written context#Not correct. Step 1: reread the embedded source instead of relying on a general preference. Step 2: locate the audience need, accuracy issue, digital tool, workflow, or publication requirement. Step 3: reject choices that weaken verification, visual accuracy, collaboration, sequence, deadline control, or publication readiness.
~D. Delay all caption checks until after the package is public#Not correct. Step 1: reread the embedded source instead of relying on a general preference. Step 2: locate the audience need, accuracy issue, digital tool, workflow, or publication requirement. Step 3: reject choices that weaken verification, visual accuracy, collaboration, sequence, deadline control, or publication readiness.
}
